'' '#line' renumbers from its own line, renames the file, and a bare '#line' keeps
'' the name in force. Every number here came off fbc first -- see the source.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/pp/src/line-directive.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered

'' The directive's own line is 100, so the line after it is 101.
'' EXPECT-OUT: const i64 101

'' A bare '#line 5' three lines later: 5 for the directive, 6 for the line after it.
'' EXPECT-OUT: const i64 6

'' 32-bit wrap, which is what fbc does with 3000000000. '__LINE__' expands to TEXT,
'' so a negative one arrives as a unary minus over the literal and the dump shows the
'' magnitude with a 'neg' beside it rather than a signed constant.
'' EXPECT-OUT: const i64 1294967295
'' EXPECT-OUT: = neg

'' '&h10' is 16; the line after it is 17.
'' EXPECT-OUT: const i64 17

'' The name is taken by BOTH '__FILE__' uses -- the second follows a '#line' with no
'' string at all, which inherits rather than reverting to line-directive.bas.
'' EXPECT-OUT: "fake.bas"
'' EXPECT-NOT: "tests/pp/src/line-directive.bas"
